  "description": "<div>\n<p>Multiply two nonnegative integers given as decimal strings and return their product as a decimal string. Do not convert either entire input to an integer or use a big-integer multiplication shortcut; accumulate digit products as in long multiplication. The output has no leading zeroes unless it is zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> num1 = \"2\", num2 = \"3\"\n<strong>Output:</strong> \"6\"\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Two times three is six.</pre>\n<p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> num1 = \"123\", num2 = \"456\"\n<strong>Output:</strong> \"56088\"\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Long multiplication gives 56,088.</pre>\n<p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n<pre><strong>Input:</strong> num1 = \"0\", num2 = \"999\"\n<strong>Output:</strong> \"0\"\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Zero times any number is zero.</pre>\n<p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>1 &lt;= num1.length, num2.length &lt;= 100</code>.</li>\n<li>Each string contains only digits and has no leading zeroes, except the string <code>\"0\"</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>",
